    {
      "id": "PBiqa8SxVP67Mwo911ZBa",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 523.8571472167969,
      "y": 398.78578186035156,
      "width": 1089,
      "height": 150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 401951907,
      "version": 14,
      "versionNonce": 56655949,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779050370688,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is Kubernetes?\nKubernetes (often called K8s) is an open-source container orchestration platform that \nautomates the deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications.\nThink of it this way: Docker lets you package an app into a container. \nBut what happens when you have 100 containers across 50 servers? Who restarts a crashed container? \nWho distributes load? Who scales when traffic spikes? That's exactly the problem Kubernetes solves.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is Kubernetes?\nKubernetes (often called K8s) is an open-source container orchestration platform that \nautomates the deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications.\nThink of it this way: Docker lets you package an app into a container. \nBut what happens when you have 100 containers across 50 servers? Who restarts a crashed container? \nWho distributes load? Who scales when traffic spikes? That's exactly the problem Kubernetes solves.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pooE2R2i_5zZm8D9qY6yO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 523.4285888671875,
      "y": 584.642822265625,
      "width": 1006.0000000000001,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2122130733,
      "version": 181,
      "versionNonce": 1084125603,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779050369089,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why was it introduced?\nBefore Kubernetes, companies running microservices at scale (Google, Netflix, etc.) faced a\npainful problem: managing containers manually doesn't scale. Google had been running\nbillions of containers a week internally using a system called Borg. In 2014, they open-\nsourced the ideas behind Borg as Kubernetes.\nProblems it solves:\n\n-Containers crashing and nobody restarting them\n-Uneven load — some servers overloaded, others idle\n-Deployments causing downtime\n-No easy way to scale up/down based on traffic\n-Service discovery (\"how does service A find service B?\")\n-Managing secrets and configuration across environments",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why was it introduced?\nBefore Kubernetes, companies running microservices at scale (Google, Netflix, etc.) faced a painful problem: managing containers manually doesn't scale. Google had been running billions of containers a week internally using a system called Borg. In 2014, they open-sourced the ideas behind Borg as Kubernetes.\nProblems it solves:\n\n-Containers crashing and nobody restarting them\n-Uneven load — some servers overloaded, others idle\n-Deployments causing downtime\n-No easy way to scale up/down based on traffic\n-Service discovery (\"how does service A find service B?\")\n-Managing secrets and configuration across environments",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
