    {
      "id": "akJePSucTRTr62XBvZBKT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1379.8307838923324,
      "y": 8229.63489173385,
      "width": 275,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aT",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 508047053,
      "version": 49,
      "versionNonce": 1105944365,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779054395454,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We will discuss each one \nof them breifly",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We will discuss each one \nof them breifly",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wrqkyxMrcGEi1_ylQ0Y7y",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 531.6021949830642,
      "y": 8612.746233703494,
      "width": 955.7426495748923,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aU",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1277276749,
      "version": 199,
      "versionNonce": 1663193219,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779054476244,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Let's understand cluster,node and pod\nThink of it like Russian nesting dolls: a Cluster contains Nodes, and Nodes contain\nPods.\n\nCluster → the entire Kubernetes system as a whole. All machines, all workloads,\nmanaged as one unit.\n\nNode → a single machine (physical server or VM) inside the cluster. It runs your\nactual application containers.\n\nPod → the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. Not a container — a wrapper around\none or more containers that share network and storage.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Let's understand cluster,node and pod\nThink of it like Russian nesting dolls: a Cluster contains Nodes, and Nodes contain Pods.\n\nCluster → the entire Kubernetes system as a whole. All machines, all workloads, managed as one unit.\n\nNode → a single machine (physical server or VM) inside the cluster. It runs your actual application containers.\n\nPod → the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. Not a container — a wrapper around one or more containers that share network and storage.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "qpz7UvgOU8VJKtJ18O7Td",
      "type": "image",
      "x": 496.0396148988182,
      "y": 8920.858479294722,
      "width": 711.0685426428956,
      "height": 395.03807924605314,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "transparent",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1344002915,
      "version": 57,
      "versionNonce": 1855803907,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779054497985,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "status": "saved",
      "fileId": "e7ce6065265be60c37e53f28e851567953398af8",
      "scale": [
        1,
        1
      ],
      "crop": null
    },
    {
      "id": "DcfkKjq-D_JyBZ9ArDTHn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 513.9763110372135,
      "y": 9323.632827451109,
      "width": 1106.4604781403632,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aW",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1347701987,
      "version": 388,
      "versionNonce": 1248746509,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779054738033,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is a Pod — really?\nA Pod is NOT just a container. Here's the critical mental model shift:\nA Pod is a logical host for one or more containers. Containers inside the same pod share:\n\n-The same network namespace → same IP address, same localhost\n-The same storage volumes → can read/write the same files\n-The same lifecycle → they start and stop together\n\nWhy does this matter? Imagine your app needs a log shipper running alongside it. They need to read\nthe same log files. You'd put both the app container and the log-shipper container in the same Pod.\nThey share the filesystem. They talk via localhost. They live and die together.\nA pod always lives on exactly one node. It cannot span nodes. If the node dies, the pod dies with it\n(and a controller will recreate it on another node).\n\n\na pod is basically a abstraction/running environment over container \n\nit's always suggested 1 application per pod for better infra and performance,but u can run more then\none container in a pod",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is a Pod — really?\nA Pod is NOT just a container. Here's the critical mental model shift:\nA Pod is a logical host for one or more containers. Containers inside the same pod share:\n\n-The same network namespace → same IP address, same localhost\n-The same storage volumes → can read/write the same files\n-The same lifecycle → they start and stop together\n\nWhy does this matter? Imagine your app needs a log shipper running alongside it. They need to read the same log files. You'd put both the app container and the log-shipper container in the same Pod. They share the filesystem. They talk via localhost. They live and die together.\nA pod always lives on exactly one node. It cannot span nodes. If the node dies, the pod dies with it (and a controller will recreate it on another node).\n\n\na pod is basically a abstraction/running environment over container \n\nit's always suggested 1 application per pod for better infra and performance,but u can run more then\none container in a pod",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
